INV-linub, python half. `io_primitives/python.yaml` declares a receiver TYPE in
the module slot of every `kind: method` primitive -- seventeen of them. The only
way the analyzer learns an external receiver's type is EXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES,
which was a hand-curated FOUR-row restatement naming three. Two homes for one
fact, drifted, so fifteen types could never be minted and every method hanging
off them was structurally unreachable:

    pathlib.Path(a).write_text(b)   -> calls python:pathlib.Path:...:write_text
    smtplib.SMTP(a).sendmail(...)   -> NO method-call edge at all
    sqlite3.Cursor(a).execute(b)    -> NO method-call edge at all

The table now READS the catalogue, and a parity test enumerates the LIVE
catalogue rather than three examples, so a type added to the YAML tomorrow fails
this test instead of silently going unreachable. A leaf name claimed by two types
is withheld (default-deny) rather than resolved to whichever came first.

